Can you explain how Boot reduces boilerplate compared to plain Spring?

Spring Boot reduces boilerplate by:
Eliminating XML (auto bean detection)
Auto-configuring based on classpath
Embedding servers (Tomcat/Jetty)
Providing starter dependencies
Convention over configuration → “It just works”

What is Inversion of Control (IoC)?
“Inversion of Control” simply means you (developer) are not creating or managing objects manually —
you let Spring create, manage, and inject them for you.
✅ Normally: Your code controls the objects.
✅ With IoC: Spring controls the objects.
That’s why it’s called Inversion of Control — the control of object creation is “inverted” from you to Spring.


What is Dependency Injection (DI)?
Dependency Injection is how Spring implements IoC.
It means giving the required object (dependency) to a class instead of the class creating it itself.

| Type                      | Example                                      | Description                                               |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Constructor Injection** | `@Autowired public Car(Engine e)`            | Best practice, ensures immutability                       |
| **Setter Injection**      | `@Autowired public void setEngine(Engine e)` | Optional dependencies                                     |
| **Field Injection**       | `@Autowired private Engine engine;`          | Quick, but less testable (not recommended for large apps) |


What is the IoC Container?
In Spring, the IoC Container is what manages all your objects (called beans).
It does three main jobs:
Creates objects (Beans)
Manages dependencies (injects them)
Controls lifecycle (init, destroy, etc.)
IoC containers in Spring:
ApplicationContext (commonly used)
BeanFactory (basic version)

| Question                                 | Best Answer                                                                                                        |
| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **What is IoC?**                         | It’s a design principle where the control of object creation and lifecycle is handed over to the Spring container. |
| **What is DI?**                          | It’s a way for Spring to inject dependencies into a class instead of the class creating them itself.               |
| **Relation between IoC and DI?**         | IoC is the principle, and DI is the technique that implements it.                                                  |
| **What is a Bean in Spring?**            | A bean is any object managed by the Spring IoC container.                                                          |
| **How does Spring know what to inject?** | Through annotations like `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@Autowired`.                                |

Beans Lifecycle:
Created → Dependencies Injected → Initialized → Used → Destroyed

| Step | Phase                                           | Description                                                                      |
| ---- | ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1️⃣  | **Instantiation**                               | Spring **creates the object** of your bean class                                 |
| 2️⃣  | **Populate properties (DI)**                    | Spring **injects dependencies** using `@Autowired`, `@Value`, etc.               |
| 3️⃣  | **BeanNameAware / BeanFactoryAware (optional)** | If your bean implements these interfaces, Spring passes context info             |
| 4️⃣  | **Before Initialization (BeanPostProcessor)**   | Custom logic before init methods                                                 |
| 5️⃣  | **Initialization**                              | Spring calls methods annotated with `@PostConstruct` or `afterPropertiesSet()`   |
| 6️⃣  | **After Initialization (BeanPostProcessor)**    | Any post-processing logic runs                                                   |
| 7️⃣  | **Bean is ready for use**                       | Bean is in the ApplicationContext — you can use it                               |
| 8️⃣  | **Destruction**                                 | When app closes, Spring calls `@PreDestroy` or `destroy()` to clean up resources |

Annotations in Bean Lifecycle
| Annotation                         | Purpose                                            | When It Runs               |
| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `@PostConstruct`                   | Initialization logic (after dependencies injected) | After object creation      |
| `@PreDestroy`                      | Cleanup logic (before bean is destroyed)           | Before app shutdown        |
| `@Autowired`                       | Dependency injection                               | During property population |
| `@Bean(initMethod, destroyMethod)` | Specify custom init/destroy methods                | On bean config             |


| Phase                | Analogy                          |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Instantiation        | Baby is born 👶                  |
| Dependency Injection | Learns from parents and teachers |
| Initialization       | Starts working / setup ready     |
| Usage                | Performs actual tasks            |
| Destruction          | Retires / passes away ☠️         |


Can you explain how Spring Boot picks environment-specific files?
Spring Boot loads:

The default file (application.properties or .yml)
Then it looks at the active profile, and loads that file on top of it
(overriding duplicate properties)

